@page "/learn/browser-options"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Reconnection settings in C# · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<style>
    .browseroptions-time { position: relative; height: 30px; margin: 2px 0 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--edge); }
    .browseroptions-tick { position: absolute; bottom: 0; width: 2px; height: 14px; background: var(--bad); }
    .browseroptions-tick.ok { background: var(--good); }
    .browseroptions-back { position: absolute; top: 0; bottom: 0; border-right: 1px dashed var(--soft); padding-right: 4px; font-size: 11px; line-height: 1; color: var(--soft); white-space: nowrap; }
    .browseroptions-axis { position: absolute; bottom: -15px; font-size: 10.5px; color: var(--soft); }
</style>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Reconnection settings in C#</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your scanners lose Wi-Fi for a few seconds. How do you make Blazor retry every 2 seconds?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>BrowserOptions</code>, Blazor's start-up settings as a C# object.
        In .NET 10 you could set the same reconnection options, but only in JavaScript, by starting Blazor yourself
        with <code>Blazor.start()</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Picture a warehouse pick list. It runs on the server, over a live connection. When the connection drops,
        Blazor tries to reconnect. By default it tries 30 times: 10 straight away, then 10 five seconds apart, then
        10 thirty seconds apart. Scanners that drop out for a few seconds need something steadier: a try every 2
        seconds, 20 times.
    </p>
    <p>
        In .NET 10 you ask for that in a <code>Blazor.start()</code> script. Misspell a name and Blazor quietly uses
        its defaults. Say a page asks for 20 tries, 2 seconds apart, but gets two names wrong:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 10 page's start-up script</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;script src="_framework/blazor.web.js" autostart="false"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
    Blazor.start({ circuit: { reconnectionOptions: {
        <span class="err">maxRetry: 20,</span>
        <span class="err">retryIntervalMs: 2000</span>
    } } });
&lt;/script&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>Here's the Wi-Fi dropping for 10 seconds:</p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 1 · .NET 10: the Wi-Fi drops for 10 seconds</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h bad">.NET 10 · Blazor.start(), two names misspelt</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li class="never" style="--d:3.3s">asks for 20 tries, 2 seconds apart</li>
                <li style="--d:0.3s">the Wi-Fi drops</li>
                <li style="--d:0.7s">10 tries straight away <span style="--d:1s">· the banner says <b>attempt 11 of 30</b></span></li>
                <li style="--d:2.1s">then one try every 5 seconds</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="browseroptions-time" aria-label="Tries in the first 12 seconds">
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.60s; left:0%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.62s; left:0.6%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.64s; left:1.2%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.66s; left:1.8%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.68s; left:2.4%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.70s; left:3%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.72s; left:3.6%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.74s; left:4.2%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.76s; left:4.8%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:0.78s; left:5.4%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:2.1s; left:50%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick" style="--d:3.35s; left:91.7%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-back" style="--d:3.1s; right:16.7%">Wi-Fi back</span>
                <span class="browseroptions-axis" style="left:0">0 s</span>
                <span class="browseroptions-axis" style="right:0">12 s</span>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict bad" style="--d:3.6s">✗ Blazor used its defaults, and nothing warned you.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            The names <code>maxRetry</code> and <code>retryIntervalMs</code> are wrong, so Blazor ignores them and
            nothing tells you. It fires 10 quick tries, then waits 5 seconds between each one.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <p>
        You can try it. The page below asks the .NET 10 way, with the two misspelt names. A yellow banner above the
        list counts the tries while the connection is down.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="Settings in a Blazor.start() script" Url="demo/browser-options/pick-list/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: Blazor.start() in JavaScript" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Drop my Wi-Fi for 10 seconds</b>. Within a second the banner says <b>Rejoin attempt 11 of
            30</b>: the 10 quick tries are already used up, and there are 30 tries, not 20. Those are Blazor's
            defaults.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>Nothing checks a JavaScript object</h2>
    <p>Here's what the page sent, beside the names Blazor reads:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The script, and what Blazor made of it</span>
        <pre class="snip">Blazor.start({ circuit: { reconnectionOptions: {
    <span class="err">maxRetry</span>: 20,            <span class="dim">// Blazor reads maxRetries</span>
    <span class="err">retryIntervalMs</span>: 2000     <span class="dim">// Blazor reads retryIntervalMilliseconds</span>
} } });                    <span class="err">both ignored: the defaults apply</span>

<span class="dim">// Settings from config: Razor pastes in a value nobody set</span>
maxRetries: ,              <span class="err">SyntaxError: Blazor never starts</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The settings are a plain JavaScript object. A wrong name is just an extra property nobody reads. And because
        the page starts Blazor itself, a broken script means Blazor never starts. The page looks fine until someone
        clicks. To see it, choose <b>Settings from config</b> in Fig. 2 and press <b>Pick</b>. Nothing happens.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Put the settings in C#</h2>
    <p>Build a <code>BrowserOptions</code> and hand it to the page with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;ConfigureBrowser Options="warehouseWifi" /&gt;</span>

static readonly BrowserOptions warehouseWifi = new()
{
    InteractiveServer =
    {
        <span class="hl">ReconnectionMaxRetries = 20,</span>
        <span class="hl">ReconnectionRetryInterval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2),</span>
    },
};</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        Now a wrong name won't compile. A value you leave out is <code>null</code>, which keeps Blazor's default.
        You don't write <code>Blazor.start()</code> at all.
    </p>

    <figure class="fig anim" data-anim>
        <figcaption class="fig-title">Fig. 3 · .NET 11: the same drop, settings in C#</figcaption>
        <button type="button" class="fig-replay" data-replay hidden>▶ Play</button>
        <div class="lane">
            <div class="lane-h ok">.NET 11 · BrowserOptions in C#</div>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li>asks for 20 tries, 2 seconds apart</li>
                <li style="--d:0.3s">the Wi-Fi drops</li>
                <li style="--d:1.1s">first try after 2 seconds <span style="--d:1.1s">· the banner says <b>1 of 20</b></span></li>
                <li style="--d:1.6s">then one try every 2 seconds</li>
                <li style="--d:3.1s">the Wi-Fi is back <span class="done" style="--d:3.3s">✓ reconnected</span></li>
            </ol>
            <div class="browseroptions-time" aria-label="Tries in the first 12 seconds">
                <span class="browseroptions-tick ok" style="--d:1.1s; left:16.7%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick ok" style="--d:1.6s; left:33.3%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick ok" style="--d:2.1s; left:50%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick ok" style="--d:2.6s; left:66.7%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-tick ok" style="--d:3.1s; left:83.3%"></span>
                <span class="browseroptions-back" style="--d:3.1s; right:16.7%">Wi-Fi back</span>
                <span class="browseroptions-axis" style="left:0">0 s</span>
                <span class="browseroptions-axis" style="right:0">12 s</span>
            </div>
            <div class="verdict ok" style="--d:3.6s">✓ 20 tries, 2 seconds apart, as asked.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="fig-cap">
            The settings are C# properties, so a wrong name won't compile. Blazor tries every 2 seconds, as asked,
            and reconnects as soon as the Wi-Fi is back.
        </p>
    </figure>

    <DemoFigure Number="4" Title="The same pick list, settings in C#" Url="demo/browser-options/pick-list"
                Label=".NET 11: BrowserOptions in C#">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Drop my Wi-Fi for 10 seconds</b>. The banner says "of 20" and counts up one try every 2
            seconds. After 10 seconds it reconnects.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here are the two pages:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Reconnect/PickList.razor" Feature="browser-options">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 4's page. The settings are a <code>static readonly</code> object, built once, and only this page gets
            them.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="6" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/Reconnect/PickListNet10.razor" Feature="browser-options">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page, for comparison, so just skim it. It's the whole HTML page, because
            <code>Blazor.start()</code> needs <code>autostart="false"</code> on the script tag. The highlighted part
            is the old way: that script tag, then the two scripts shown above. The rest, like the <code>From</code>
            parameter and the in-memory config, only runs the demo.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Start Blazor yourself</h2>
    <p>We tried three versions of the script on .NET 10, and dropped the connection each time:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three Blazor.start() scripts in .NET 10, and what the browser did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">maxRetries: 20,
retryIntervalMilliseconds: 2000</pre>
                <span class="why">"1 of 20, 2 of 20…", trying again in 2 seconds</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ works, if every name is right</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="err">maxRetry</span>: 20,
<span class="err">retryIntervalMs</span>: 2000</pre>
                <span class="why">"Rejoin attempt 11 of 30" after a second, then a try every 5 seconds</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the defaults, with no warning</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">maxRetries:
  @@Config["Reconnect:Tries"],</pre>
                <span class="why">renders <code>maxRetries: ,</code>: "SyntaxError: Unexpected token ','"</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ Blazor never starts</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Even the script that worked had a cost. You had to turn off autostart in <code>App.razor</code> and keep the
        settings in JavaScript, where nothing checks them. If your site has a strict security policy, you also had to
        loosen it so a script written into the page could run.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Settings in C#</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the same settings are C# properties. Set them for one page with <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>,
        as Fig. 4 does, or for every page with <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> in <code>Program.cs</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        Reading them from config is safe too, because there's no script for a missing value to break. Read the value
        as <code>int?</code>. A missing key gives <code>null</code>, which keeps Blazor's default. The demos don't
        show this case.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Settings from config, in .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip">ReconnectionMaxRetries = <span class="hl">config.GetValue&lt;int?&gt;("Reconnect:Tries")</span>,  <span class="dim">// not set: null, Blazor's default</span></pre>
    </div>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Don't use both, at least in RC3.</b> This guide runs on .NET 11 RC3, a preview, so this may change. In RC2 and RC3, once <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> is set, <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code> writes into the app-wide settings. One visit to one page would change every page, for everyone.</li>
        <li><b>Only pages with an interactive component get them.</b> The settings are written into the page just before its first Server, WebAssembly or Auto component.</li>
        <li><b>Code still needs JavaScript.</b> C# settings can't hold functions. So a retry-interval function, <code>configureSignalR</code> or your own <code>reconnectionHandler</code> stays in <code>Blazor.start()</code>, or in a JS initializer (a script Blazor runs as it starts).</li>
        <li><b>The server end has its own settings.</b> The live connection ends at a SignalR hub on the server. To set the hub's options, use <code>AddInteractiveServerRenderMode(o =&gt; o.ConfigureConnection = …)</code>, also new in .NET 11.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
